\begin_layout Title
Chapter 3: Consumer theory
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Need to complete: Problem 3, Section 3.4.4
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
3.4
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Consumer Theory III: Choice
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In this section, we investigate agents' choice in a competitive environment
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
3.4.1
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Competitive environment
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Competitive Budgets
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The set of alternatives 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 has the same structure as in the consumer's preference model, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $X\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

.
 We will assume for simplicity that 
\begin_inset Formula $X\equiv\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
In addition to the physical constraints embodied in the consumption set,
 the consumer's consumtion choice is limited to those commodity bundles
 that he can afford.
 We suppose that the 
\begin_inset Formula $L$
\end_inset

 commodities are all traded in the market at dollar prices that are publicly
 quoted (this is the 
\emph on
principle of completeness
\emph default
, or 
\emph on
universality
\emph default
, 
\emph on
of markets
\emph default
).
 Formally these prices are represented by the 
\emph on
price vector
\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
We also assume that these prices are beyond the influence of the consumer.
 This is the so-called 
\emph on
price-taking assumption
\emph default
.
 This assumption is likely to be valid when the consumer's deman for any
 commodity represents only a small fraction of the total demand for that
 good.
 The affordability of a consumption bundle depends on the market prices
 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

 and the consumer's wealth level (in dollars) 
\begin_inset Formula $w\in\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The 
\emph on
Walrasian
\emph default
, or 
\emph on
competitive budget
\emph default
, set 
\begin_inset Formula $B(p,w)\equiv\{x\in X:p\cdot x\leq w\}$
\end_inset

 is the set of all feasible consumption bundles for the consumer who faces
 market prices 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 and has wealth 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

.
 We consider agents' choices from the family of competitive (Walrasian)
 budget sets 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{B}^{W}\equiv\{B(p,w):p\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L},\ w\in\mathbb{R}_{+}\}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The set 
\begin_inset Formula $\{x\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}:p\cdot x=w\}$
\end_inset

 is called the 
\emph on
budget hyperplane
\emph default
.
 It determines the upper boundary of the budget set.
 The price vector 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

, drawn starting from any point 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{x}$
\end_inset

 on the budget hyperplane, must be orthogonal (perpendicular) to any vector
 starting at 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{x}$
\end_inset

 and lying on the budget hyperplane.
 This is so because for any 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{\prime}$
\end_inset

that itself lies on the budget hyperplane, we have 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x^{\prime}=p\cdot\bar{x}=w$
\end_inset

.
 Hence, 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot\Delta x=0$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta x=(x-x^{\prime})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The Walrasian budget set 
\begin_inset Formula $B(p,w)$
\end_inset

 is a 
\emph on
convex 
\emph default
set: That is, if bundles 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{\prime}$
\end_inset

are both elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $B(p,w)$
\end_inset

, then the bundle 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{\prime\prime}=\alpha x+(1-\alpha)x^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 is also.
 To see this, note first that because both 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 are nonnegative, 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{\prime\prime}\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

.
 Second, since 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x\leq w$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x^{\prime}\leq w$
\end_inset

, we have 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x^{\prime\prime}=\alpha(p\cdot x)+(1-\alpha)(p\cdot x^{\prime})\leq w$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{\prime\prime}\in B(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The convexity of 
\begin_inset Formula $B(p,w)$
\end_inset

 plays a significant role in the development that follows.
 Note that the convexity of 
\begin_inset Formula $B(p,w)$
\end_inset

 depends on the convexity of the consumption set 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

.
 With a more general consumption set 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $B(p,w)$
\end_inset

 will be convex as long as 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 is.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Walrasian Choice Structure
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The 
\emph on
consumer's 
\emph default
problem
\emph on
, 
\emph default
given prices 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 and wealth 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

, can thus be stated as follows: 
\emph on
Choose a consumption bundles
\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 
\emph on
from 
\begin_inset Formula $B(p,w)$
\end_inset


\emph default
.
 An agent in a competitive environment is represented by a Walrasian choice
 structure 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathcal{B}^{W},C)$
\end_inset

.
 Choice in this environment is interpreted as demand.
 Thus, we refer to 
\begin_inset Formula $C$
\end_inset

 as the agent's demand correspondence.
 In order to simplify notation, we write 
\begin_inset Formula $C(p,w)$
\end_inset

 instead of 
\begin_inset Formula $C(B(p,w))$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
C:\mathcal{B}^{W}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Choice structure 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathcal{B},C)$
\end_inset

 is single-valued if for each 
\begin_inset Formula $B\in\mathcal{B}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $C(B)$
\end_inset

 is a singleton.
 Each single-valued choice structure induces a demand function.
 The demand function associated with a single-valued choice structure 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathcal{B}^{W},C)$
\end_inset

, denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

, is given by: for each 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,w)\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)$
\end_inset

 is the unique element in 
\begin_inset Formula $C(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In what follows, whenever we refer to a choice structure, we implicitly
 assume that it is a competitive choice structure, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathcal{B}^{W},C)$
\end_inset

.
 Whenever we refer to a demand function, we implicitly assume that there
 is a single-valued choice structure that generates it.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Are there sensible restrictions on choice if we interpret commodities as
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

goods
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

? We maintain two assumptions regarding the Walrasian demand correspondence
 
\begin_inset Formula $C(p,w)$
\end_inset

: That it is 
\emph on
homogeneous of degree zero 
\emph default
and that is satifies 
\emph on
Walras' law
\emph default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(58).

\series default
 
\emph on
A function 
\begin_inset Formula $f:\mathbb{R}_{+}^{M}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 is 
\series bold
homogeneous of degree 
\begin_inset Formula $r\in\mathbb{N}$
\end_inset

 
\series default
iff 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \alpha\in\mathbb{R}_{+},\ \forall\ z\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{M}:f(\alpha z)=\alpha^{r}f(z)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The Walrasian demand correspondence 
\begin_inset Formula $C(p,w)$
\end_inset

 is 
\emph on
homogenous
\emph default
 
\emph on
of degree zero
\emph default
 iff 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \{p,w\}\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{+},\ \forall\ \alpha\in\mathbb{R}_{++}:C(\alpha p,\alpha w)=C(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
 Homogeneity of degree zero says that if both prices and wealth change in
 the same proportion, then the individual's consumption choice does not
 change.
 To understand this property, note that a change in prices and wealth from
 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,w)$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $(\alpha p,\alpha w)$
\end_inset

 leads to no change in the consumer's set of feasible consumption bundles;
 that is, 
\begin_inset Formula $B(p,w)=B(\alpha p,\alpha w)$
\end_inset

.
 Homogeneity of degree zero says that the individual's choice depends only
 on the set of feasible points.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

One convenient implication of 
\begin_inset Formula $C(p,w)$
\end_inset

 being homogenous of degree zero can be noted immediately: Although 
\begin_inset Formula $C(p,w)$
\end_inset

 formally has 
\begin_inset Formula $L+1$
\end_inset

 arguments, we can, with no loss of generality, fix (
\emph on
normalize
\emph default
) the level of one of the 
\begin_inset Formula $L+1$
\end_inset

 independent variables at an arbitrary level.
 One common normalization is 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{l}=1$
\end_inset

 for some 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

.
 Another is 
\begin_inset Formula $w=1$
\end_inset

.Hence, the effective number of arguments in 
\begin_inset Formula $C(p,w)$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $L$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(59).

\series default
 
\emph on
A choice structure 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathcal{B}^{W},C)$
\end_inset


\emph default
 
\emph on
satisfies Walras' law iff 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ (p,w)\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{+},\ \forall\ x\in C(p,w):p\cdot x=w$
\end_inset

.
 A demand function 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 satisfies Walras' law iff 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ (p,w)\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{+}:p\cdot x(p,w)=w$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Walras' law says that the consumer fully expends his wealth.
 Intuitively, this is a reasonable assumption to make as long as there is
 some good that is clearly desirable.
 Walras' law should be understood broadly: the consumer's budget may be
 an intertemporal one allowing for savings today to be used for purchases
 tomorrow.
 What Walras' law says is that the consumer fully expends his resources
 
\emph on
over his lifetime
\emph default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
3.4.2
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Comparative statics
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We are often interested in analyzing how the consumer's choice varies with
 changes in his wealth and in prices.
 The examination of a change in outcome in response to a change in underlying
 economic parameters is known as 
\emph on
comparative
\emph default
 
\emph on
statics
\emph default
 analysis.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Wealth effects
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(60).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 be a demand function and 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

.
 The 
\series bold
wealth expansion path (or Engle function) for 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 at 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset


\series default
 is the function: 
\begin_inset Formula $w\in\mathbb{R}_{+}\mapsto x(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
 We usually denote this function 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,\cdot)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

For fixed prices 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{p}$
\end_inset

, the function 
\begin_inset Formula $x(\bar{p},w)$
\end_inset

 is called the consumer's 
\emph on
Engel function
\emph default
.
 Its image in 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{\bar{p}}=\{x(\bar{p},w):w>0\}$
\end_inset

, is known as the 
\emph on
wealth
\emph default
, or 
\emph on
income
\emph default
,
\emph on
 expansion path
\emph default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

At any 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,w)$
\end_inset

, the derivative 
\begin_inset Formula $\nicefrac{\partial x_{l}(p,w)}{\partial w}$
\end_inset

 is known as the 
\emph on
wealth
\emph default
 
\emph on
effect
\emph default
 for the 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

th good.
 It is also known as the 
\emph on
income
\emph default
 
\emph on
effect
\emph default
 in the literature.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(61) and (62).
 
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 be a differentiable demand function.
 
\series bold
Demand for commodity 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 is normal at 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,w)\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset


\series default
 iff 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\partial x_{l}}{\partial w}(p,w)>0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
Demand 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is normal
\series default
 iff 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ p\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L},\ \forall\ \{w,w^{\prime}\}\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{+}:w\geq w^{\prime}\implies x(p,w)\geq x(p,w^{\prime})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
The assumption of normal demand makes sense if commodities are large aggregates
 (e.g., food, shelter).
 But if they are very disaggregated (e.g., particular kinds of shoes), then
 because of substitution to higher-quality goods as wealth increase, goods
 that become inferiro at some level of wealth may be the rule rather than
 the exception.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In matrix notation, the wealth effects are represented as follows:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(63).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 be a differentiable demand function.
 The 
\series bold
matrix of wealth effects at 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,w)\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset


\series default
 is the partial derivative of 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 with respect to wealth, i.e.,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
D_{w}x(p,w)\equiv\left[\begin{array}{c}
\frac{\partial x_{1}}{\partial w}(p,w)\\
\vdots\\
\frac{\partial x_{L}}{\partial w}(p,w)
\end{array}\right]\in\mathbb{R}^{L}
\]

\end_inset

(This is the derivative, not the gradient)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Price effects
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We can also ask how consumption levels of the various commodities change
 as prices vary.
 The derivative 
\begin_inset Formula $\nicefrac{\partial x_{l}(p,w)}{\partial p_{k}}$
\end_inset

 is known as the 
\emph on
price effect of 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{k}$
\end_inset


\emph default
, the price of good 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

, 
\emph on
on the demand for good 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset


\emph default
.
 Although it may be natural to think that a fall in a good's price will
 lead to the consumer to purchase more of it, the reverse situation is not
 an economic impossibility.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(64).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 be a differentiable demand function.
 
\series bold
Commodity 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 is a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Giffen good
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 at 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,w)\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset


\series default
 iff 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\partial x_{l}}{\partial p_{l}}(p,w)>0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Low-quality goods may well be Giffen goods for consumers with low wealth
 levels.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\series default
The price effects are conveniently represented in matrix form as follows:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(65).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 be a differentiable demand function.
 The 
\series bold
matrix of price effects at 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,w)\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

 
\series default
is the matrix of partial derivatives of 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 with respect to prices, i.e.,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
D_{p}x(p,w)\equiv\left[\begin{array}{ccc}
\frac{\partial x_{1}}{\partial p_{1}}(p,w) & \cdots & \frac{\partial x_{1}}{\partial p_{L}}(p,w)\\
\vdots &  & \vdots\\
\frac{\partial x_{L}(p,w)}{\partial p_{1}} & \cdots & \frac{\partial x_{L}}{\partial p_{L}}(p,w)
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset

(This is the derivative, not the gradient)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Note*
We use derivative matrices for linearization and approximation.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $f:\mathbb{R}^{N}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{M}$
\end_inset

is a vector-valued differentiable function, then at any 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in\mathbb{R}^{N}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $Df(x)$
\end_inset

 is the 
\begin_inset Formula $M\times N$
\end_inset

 matrix whose mn-th entry is 
\begin_inset Formula $\nicefrac{\partial f_{m}(x)}{\partial x_{n}}$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $M=1,$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $Df(x)$
\end_inset

 is a 
\begin_inset Formula $1\times N$
\end_inset

 matrix.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
dimenstion of the numerator (row) 
\begin_inset Formula $\times$
\end_inset

 dimenstion of the denominator (column)
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Note*
The gradient vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\nabla f(\bar{x})\in\mathbb{R}^{N}$
\end_inset

 of a function at a point 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{x}$
\end_inset

 is the vector whose nth entry is the partial derivative with respect to
 the nth variable of the real-valued function, 
\begin_inset Formula $f:\mathbb{R}^{N}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

, evaluated at the point 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{N}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\family roman
\series medium
\shape up
\size normal
\emph off
\bar no
\strikeout off
\uuline off
\uwave off
\noun off
\color none
\begin_inset Formula $\nabla f(x)=\left[Df(x)\right]^{T}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\family roman
\series medium
\shape up
\size normal
\emph off
\bar no
\strikeout off
\uuline off
\uwave off
\noun off
\color none
The Hessian Matrix: 
\begin_inset Formula $D^{2}f(x)=D\left[\nabla f(x)\right]$
\end_inset

.
 The Hessian Matrix is the derivative matrix of the vector-valued gradient
 function.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Elasticities
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Elasticity with respect to wealth
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(66).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 be a differentibale demand function.
 The 
\series bold
elasticity of demand for commodity 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 with respect to wealth at 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,w)\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset


\series default
 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\epsilon_{lw}(p,w)\equiv\frac{\partial x_{l}(p,w)}{\partial w}\frac{w}{x_{l}(p,w)}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Luxury
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(67).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 be a differentiable demand function.
 Commodity 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 is a 
\series bold
luxury for 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 at 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,w)\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset


\series default
 iff the elasticity of demand for commodity 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 with respect to wealth at 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,w)$
\end_inset

 is greater than or equal to 1.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Elasticity with respect to price
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(68).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 be a differentiable demand function.
 The 
\series bold
elasticity of demand for commodity 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 with respect to price of commodity 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 at 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,w)\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset


\series default
 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\epsilon_{lk}(p,w)\equiv\frac{\partial x_{l}(p,w)}{\partial p_{k}}\frac{p_{k}}{x_{l}(p,w)}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
These elasticities give the 
\emph on
percentage 
\emph default
change in demand for good 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 per (marginal) percentage change in price of good 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 or wealth.
 Elasticities arise very frequently in applied work.
 Unlike the derivatives of demand, elasticies are independent of the units
 chosen for measuring commodities and therefore provide a unit-free way
 of capturing demand responsiveness.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
3.4.3
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

WARP
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In this section, we study the implicaitons of the weak axiom of revealed
 preference for consumer demand.
 The weak axiom was introduced in Section 2.3 as a consistency axiom for
 the choice-based approach to decision theory In this section, we explore
 its implicaitons for the demand behavior of a consumer.
 In the preference-based approach to consumer behavior to be studied in
 the next two sections, demand necessarily satisfies the weak axiom.
 Thus, the results presented in the upcoming sections, when compared with
 those in this section, will tell us how much more structure is imposed
 on consumer demand by the preference-based approach beyond what is implied
 by the weak axiom in conjunction with homogeneity of degree zero and Walras'
 law.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The following proposition states that a single-valued choice structure satisfies
 WARP if and only if for each two different budget sets in which the choices
 are different, the choices cannot be both in the intersection of the sets.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*

\series bold
\shape up
(10).

\series default
 
\emph on
A single-valued Walrasian choice structure 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathcal{B}^{W},C)$
\end_inset

 satisfies WARP if and only if its associated demand function 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 satisfies the following property: for any two price-wealth combinations,
 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,w)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $(p^{\prime},w^{\prime})$
\end_inset

, if 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[p\cdot x(p^{\prime},w^{\prime})\leq w\ \mbox{and}\ x(p^{\prime},w^{\prime})\neq x(p,w)\right]$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[p^{\prime}\cdot x(p,w)>w^{\prime}\right]$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathcal{B}^{W},C)$
\end_inset

 be a single-valued Walrasian choice structure with demand function 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $(\Rightarrow)$
\end_inset

 Assume the choice structure satisfies WARP.
 We want to prove 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 satisfies the following property: 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \{(p,w),(p^{\prime},w^{\prime})\}\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{+}:[p\cdot x(p^{\prime},w^{\prime})\leq w\ \wedge\ x(p^{\prime},w^{\prime})\neq x(p,w)]\implies p^{\prime}\cdot x(p,w)>w^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 Take 
\begin_inset Formula $\{(p,w),(p^{\prime},w^{\prime})\}\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

.
 Suppose that 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x(p^{\prime},w^{\prime})\leq w$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p^{\prime},w^{\prime})\neq x(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
 We need to prove that 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\prime}\cdot x(p,w)>w^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 Suppose for contradiction that 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\prime}\cdot x(p,w)\leq w^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $A\equiv B(p,w)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B\equiv B(p^{\prime},w^{\prime})$
\end_inset

.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $\{A,B\}\subseteq\mathcal{B}^{W}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)\in C(A)$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p^{\prime},w^{\prime})\in C(B)$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x(p^{\prime},w^{\prime})\leq w$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p^{\prime},w^{\prime})\in A$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\prime}\cdot x(p,w)\leq w^{\prime}$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)\in B$
\end_inset

.
 By definition, 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)\in A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p^{\prime},w^{\prime})\in B$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathcal{B}^{W},C)$
\end_inset

 satisfies WARP, then 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p^{\prime},w^{\prime})\in C(A)$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p^{\prime},w^{\prime})\neq x(p,w)$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $C(A)$
\end_inset

 is not single valued.
 This is a contradiction 
\begin_inset Formula $(\rightarrow\leftarrow)$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\prime}\cdot x(p,w)>w^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $(\Leftarrow)$
\end_inset

 Assume 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 satisfies the following property: 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \{(p,w),(p^{\prime},w^{\prime})\}\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{+}:[p\cdot x(p^{\prime},w^{\prime})\leq w\ \wedge\ x(p^{\prime},w^{\prime})\neq x(p,w)]\implies p^{\prime}\cdot x(p,w)>w^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 We prove that 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathcal{B}^{W},C)$
\end_inset

 satisfies WARP.
 Suppose for contradiction that 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathcal{B}^{W},C)$
\end_inset

 does not satisfy WARP, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\exists\ \{A,B\}\subseteq\mathcal{B}^{W},\ \exists\ \{x,y\}\subseteq A\cap B:[x\in C(A)\ \wedge\ y\in C(B)]\ \wedge\ x\notin C(B)$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $x\notin C(B)$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $x\neq y$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\{A,B\}\subseteq\mathcal{B}^{W}$
\end_inset

, then there are 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,w)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $(p^{\prime},w^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $A\equiv B(p,w)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B\equiv B(p^{\prime},w^{\prime})$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in C(A)$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)=x$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $y\in C(B)$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p^{\prime},w^{\prime})=y$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $y\in A$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x(p^{\prime},w^{\prime})\leq w$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x(p^{\prime},w^{\prime})\leq w$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)\neq x(p^{\prime},w^{\prime})$
\end_inset

, then by the assumption, 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\prime}\cdot x(p,w)>w^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $x=x(p,w)\notin B$
\end_inset

.
 This is a contradiction 
\begin_inset Formula $(\rightarrow\leftarrow)$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathcal{B}^{W},C)$
\end_inset

 satisfies WARP.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In the consumer demand setting, the idea behind the weak axiom can be put
 as follows: If 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x(p^{\prime},w^{\prime})\leq w$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p^{\prime},w^{\prime})\neq x(p,w)$
\end_inset

, then we know that when facing price 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 and wealth 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

, the consumer chose consumption bundle 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)$
\end_inset

 even though bundle 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p^{\prime},w^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 was also affordable.
 We can interpret this choice as 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

revealing
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 a preference for 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)$
\end_inset

 over 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p^{\prime},w^{\prime})$
\end_inset

.
 Now, we might reasonably expect the consumer to display some consistency
 in his demand behavior.
 In particular, given his revealed preference, we expect that he would choose
 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)$
\end_inset

 over 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p^{\prime},w^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 whenever they are both affordable.
 If so, bundle 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)$
\end_inset

 must not be affordable at the price-wealth combination 
\begin_inset Formula $(p^{\prime},w^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 at which the consumer chooses bundle 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p^{\prime},w^{\prime})$
\end_inset

.
 That is, as required by the weak axiom, we must have 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\prime}\cdot x(p,w)>w^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Implications of the Weak Axiom
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The weak axiom has significant implicaitons for the effects of price changes
 on demand.
 We need to concentrate, however, on a special kind of price change.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Price changes affect the consumer in two ways.
 First, they alter the relative cost of different commodities.
 But, second, they also change the consumer's real wealth: An increase in
 the price of a commodity impoverishes the consumers of the commodity.
 To study the implicaitons of the weak axiom, we need to isolate the first
 effect.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

One way to accomplish this is to imagine a situation in which a change in
 prices is accompanied by a change in the consumer's wealth that makes his
 initial consumption bundle just affordable at the new prices.
 That is, if the consumer is originally facing prices 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 and wealth 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

 and chooses consumption bundle 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)$
\end_inset

, then when prices change to 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\prime}$
\end_inset

, we imagine that the consumer's wealth is adjusted to 
\begin_inset Formula $w^{\prime}=p^{\prime}\cdot x(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, the wealth adjustment is 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta w=\Delta p\cdot x(p,w)$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta p=(p^{\prime}-p)$
\end_inset

.
 This kind of wealth adjustment is known as 
\emph on
Slutsky wealth compensation
\emph default
.
 Geometrically, the restriction is that the budget hyperplane corresponding
 to 
\begin_inset Formula $(p^{\prime},w^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 goes through the vector 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We refer to price changes that are accompanied by such compensating wealth
 changes as (
\emph on
Slutsky
\emph default
) 
\emph on
compensated price changes
\emph default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In Proposition 11, we show that the weak axiom can be equivalently stated
 in terms of the demand response to compensated price changes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*

\series bold
\shape up
(11) (Compensated Law of Demand).

\series default
 
\emph on
A single-valued Walrasian choice structure that satisfies Walras' law satisfies
 WARP if and only if its associated demand function 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 satisfies the following property, known as the Compensated Law of Demand:
 for each 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,w)\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

 and each 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\prime}\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left[p^{\prime}-p\right]\cdot\left[x(p^{\prime},w^{\prime})-x(p,w)\right]\leq0
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $w^{\prime}\equiv p^{\prime}\cdot x(p,w)$
\end_inset

, and this inequality is strict whenever 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathcal{B}^{W},C)$
\end_inset

 be a single-valued Walrasian choice structure that satisfies Walras' law,
 and let 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 be its associated demand function.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $(\Rightarrow)$
\end_inset

 Assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathcal{B}^{W},C)$
\end_inset

 satisfies WARP.
 The result we wish to prove is immediate if 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p^{\prime},w^{\prime})=x(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
 So, Suppose that 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)\neq x(p^{\prime},w^{\prime})$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $w^{\prime}=p^{\prime}\cdot x(p,w)$
\end_inset

, then
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left[p^{\prime}-p\right]\cdot\left[x(p^{\prime},w^{\prime})-x(p,w)\right]=p\cdot x(p,w)-p\cdot x(p^{\prime},w^{\prime})
\]

\end_inset

Thus, it is enough to prove that 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x(p,w)<p\cdot x(p^{\prime},w^{\prime})$
\end_inset

.
 Suppose by means of contradiction that 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x(p,w)\geq p\cdot x(p^{\prime},w^{\prime})$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 satisfies Walras' law, then 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x(p,w)=w$
\end_inset

.
 Now, 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x(p^{\prime},w^{\prime})\leq w$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)\neq x(p^{\prime},w^{\prime})$
\end_inset

.
 Hence, by Proposition 10, 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\prime}\cdot x(p,w)>w^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $w^{\prime}>w^{\prime}$
\end_inset

, but this is a contradiction 
\begin_inset Formula $(\rightarrow\leftarrow)$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x(p,w)<p\cdot x(p^{\prime},w^{\prime})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $(\Leftarrow)$
\end_inset

 Assume 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 satisfies the CLD.
 We prove that 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathcal{B}^{W},C)$
\end_inset

 satisfies WARP.
 Suppose for contradiction that 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathcal{B}^{W},C)$
\end_inset

 does not satisfy WARP.
 Then, by Proposition 10, this is equivalent to the statement 
\begin_inset Formula $\exists\ \{(p,w),(p^{\prime},w^{\prime})\}\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{+}:\left[p\cdot x(p^{\prime},w^{\prime})\leq w\ \wedge\ x(p^{\prime},w^{\prime})\neq x(p,w)\right]\ \wedge\ p^{\prime}\cdot x(p,w)\leq w^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 To simplify notation, define 
\begin_inset Formula $x\equiv x(p,w)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{\prime}\equiv x(p^{\prime},w^{\prime})$
\end_inset

.
 Important to note, if one of these two inequalities holds with equality,
 then this is actually a compensated price change and we are done.
 So, suppose that we have 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x^{\prime}<w$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\prime}\cdot x<w^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 Let
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\alpha=\frac{w^{\prime}-p^{\prime}\cdot x}{\left[w^{\prime}-p^{\prime}\cdot x\right]+\left[w-p\cdot x^{\prime}\right]}
\]

\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x^{\prime}<w$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\prime}\cdot x<w^{\prime}$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha\in(0,1)$
\end_inset

.
 Rearranging terms yields
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\alpha w+(1-\alpha)p^{\prime}\cdot x=\alpha p\cdot x^{\prime}+(1-\alpha)w^{\prime}
\]

\end_inset

By Walras' law, 
\begin_inset Formula $w=p\cdot x$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $w^{\prime}=p^{\prime}\cdot x^{\prime}$
\end_inset

, so we obtain
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\tilde{p}\cdot x=\tilde{p}\cdot x^{\prime}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{p}\equiv\left[\alpha p+(1-\alpha)p^{\prime}\right]$
\end_inset

.
 Now, let 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{w}=\tilde{p}\cdot x=\tilde{p}\cdot x^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $w=p\cdot x$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\prime}\cdot x<w^{\prime}$
\end_inset

, then
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\alpha w+(1-\alpha)w^{\prime}>\tilde{w}
\]

\end_inset

By Walras' Law, 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{w}=\tilde{p}\cdot\tilde{x}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{x}\equiv x(\tilde{p},\tilde{w})$
\end_inset

.
 As a result,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\alpha w+(1-\alpha)w^{\prime}>\tilde{p}\cdot\tilde{x}
\]

\end_inset

By the definition of 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{p}$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\alpha w+(1-\alpha)w^{\prime}>\alpha p\cdot\tilde{x}+(1-\alpha)p^{\prime}\cdot\tilde{x}
\]

\end_inset

This strict inequality implies that we must have 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot\tilde{x}<w$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\prime}\cdot\tilde{x}<w^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 Without loss of generality, let 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot\tilde{x}<w$
\end_inset

.
 Then, we also have 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{x}\neq x$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{p}\cdot x=\tilde{w}$
\end_inset

.
 Now, consider
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
(\tilde{p}-p)\cdot(\tilde{x}-x) & = & -p\cdot(\tilde{x}-x)\\
 & = & -p\cdot\tilde{x}+p\cdot x
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

since 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{p}\cdot\tilde{x}=\tilde{w}=\tilde{p}\cdot x$
\end_inset

.
 By Walras' Law, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
-p\cdot\tilde{x}+w
\]

\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot\tilde{x}<w$
\end_inset

, then
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
-p\cdot\tilde{x}+w\geq0
\]

\end_inset

Thus, because 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{p}\cdot x=\tilde{w}$
\end_inset

 represents a compensated price change, 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 does not satisfy the CLD, but this contradicts our initial assumption 
\begin_inset Formula $(\rightarrow\leftarrow)$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathcal{B}^{W},C)$
\end_inset

 satisfies WARP.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Remark*
WARP holds if and only if it holds for all 
\emph on
compensated
\emph default
 price changes.
 That is, the weak axiom holds if, for any two price-wealth pairs 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,w)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $(p^{\prime},w^{\prime})$
\end_inset

, we have 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x(p^{\prime},w^{\prime})>w$
\end_inset

 whenever 
\begin_inset Formula $w^{\prime}=p^{\prime}\cdot x(p,w)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p^{\prime},w^{\prime})\neq x(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Note*
Suppose the consumer is originally facing prices 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 and wealth 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

, and he chooses consumption bundle 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
 Then, when prices change to 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\prime}$
\end_inset

, the consumer's wealth is adjusted to 
\begin_inset Formula $w^{\prime}=p^{\prime}\cdot x(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, the wealth adjustment is 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta w=\Delta p\cdot x(p,w)$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta p=(p^{\prime}-p)$
\end_inset

.
 We are isolating the substitution effect of a price change by eliminating
 the wealth effects.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The inequality of Proposition 11 can be written in shorthand as 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta p\cdot\Delta x\leq0$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta p=(p^{\prime}-p)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta x=\left[x(p,w)-x(p^{\prime},w^{\prime})\right]$
\end_inset

.
 It can be interpreted as a form of the 
\emph on
law of demand
\emph default
: 
\emph on
Demand and price move in opposite directions
\emph default
.
 Proposition 11 tells us that the law of demand holds for 
\emph on
compensated
\emph default
 price changes.
 We therefore call it the 
\emph on
compensated
\emph default
 
\emph on
law of demand
\emph default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The simplest case involves the effect on demand for some good 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 of a compensated change in its own price 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{l}$
\end_inset

.
 When only this price changes, we have 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta p=(0,\ldots,0,\Delta p_{l},0,\ldots,0)$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta p\cdot\Delta x=\Delta p_{l}\Delta x_{l}$
\end_inset

, then Proposition 11 tells us that if 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta p_{l}>0$
\end_inset

, then we must have 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta x_{l}<0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Note that the weak axiom is not sufficient to yield the law of demand for
 price changes that are 
\emph on
not 
\emph default
compenstated.
 A price change from 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 is obtained by a decrease in the price of good 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

, but the weak axiom imposes no restriction on where we place the new consumptio
n bundle.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
3.4.4
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Problems
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
Prove Propositions 2.E.1, 2.E.2, 2.E.3, and Exercise 2.E.2 in Mas-Colell (1995).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*

\series bold
\shape up
2.E.1.

\series default
 
\shape default
If the Walrasian demand function 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)$
\end_inset

 is homogenuous of degree zero, then for all 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \sum_{k=1}^{L}\frac{\partial x_{l}(p,w)}{\partial p_{k}}p_{k}+\frac{\partial x_{l}(p,w)}{\partial w}w=0\ \mbox{for}\ l=1,\ldots,L}
\]

\end_inset

In matrix notation, this is expressed as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
D_{p}x(p,w)p+D_{w}x(p,w)w=0
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Assume the Walrasian demand function, 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)$
\end_inset

, is homogeneous of degree zero, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \alpha>0:x(\alpha p,\alpha w)=x(p,w)$
\end_inset

, which is equivlanet to
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\forall\ \alpha>0:x(\alpha p,\alpha w)-x(p,w)=0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

To clarify the notation, we can see that 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x(\alpha p,\alpha w)=\left[\begin{array}{c}
x_{1}(\alpha p_{1},\ldots,\alpha p_{L},\alpha w)\\
\vdots\\
x_{L}(\alpha p_{1},\ldots,\alpha p_{L},\alpha w)
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Now, define a function 
\begin_inset Formula $f:\mathbb{R}_{+}\longrightarrow\mbox{\mathbb{R}}_{+}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $f(\alpha)=(\alpha p,\alpha w)$
\end_inset

.
 So, the demand function can be written as 
\begin_inset Formula $x(\alpha p,\alpha w)=x(f(\alpha))$
\end_inset

.
 Differentiating (1) with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

 yields:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \sum_{k=1}^{L}\frac{\partial x_{l}(\alpha p,\alpha w)}{\partial p_{k}}p_{k}+\frac{\partial x_{l}(\alpha p,\alpha w)}{\partial w}w=0;\ \forall\ l=1,\ldots,L}
\]

\end_inset

Important to note, we used the chain rule to obtain the desired result.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
f:\mathbb{R}_{+}\longrightarrow\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{+}\\
x_{l}:\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{+}\longrightarrow\mathbb{R}_{+}\\
\left(x_{l}\circ f\right):\mathbb{R}_{+}\longrightarrow\mathbb{R}_{+}
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(p_{1}=\alpha p_{1},\ldots,\ p_{L}=\alpha p_{L},\ w=\alpha w\right)$
\end_inset

.
 Taking the derivative of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{l}$
\end_inset

 with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

 yields
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\partial x_{l}(f(\alpha))}{\partial\alpha}=\frac{\partial x_{l}}{\partial p_{1}}\frac{\partial p_{1}}{\partial\alpha}+\ldots+\frac{\partial x_{l}}{\partial p_{L}}\frac{\partial p_{L}}{\partial\alpha}+\frac{\partial x_{l}}{\partial w}\frac{\partial w}{\partial\alpha};\ \forall\ l=1,\ldots,L
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In matrix notation, the chain rule is applied as follows:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
D_{\alpha}(x\circ f)=Dx(f(\alpha))Df(\alpha)=Dx(\alpha p,\alpha w)\left[\begin{array}{c}
p\\
w
\end{array}\right]=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
D_{p}x(\alpha p,\alpha w) & D_{w}x(\alpha p,\alpha w)\end{array}\right]\left[\begin{array}{c}
p\\
w
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset

i.e.,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\partial x(\alpha p,\alpha w)}{\partial\alpha}=\frac{\partial x(\alpha p,\alpha w)}{\partial p}\frac{\partial p}{\partial\alpha}+\frac{\partial x(\alpha p,\alpha w)}{\partial w}\frac{\partial w}{\partial\alpha}
\]

\end_inset

The final result, in matrix notation, is expressed as:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
D_{p}x(\alpha p,\alpha w)p+D_{w}x(\alpha p,\alpha w)w=0
\]

\end_inset

Evaluate at 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha=1$
\end_inset

 and we obtain our desired result.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*

\series bold
\shape up
2.E.2.

\series default
 
\shape default
If the Walrasian demand function 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)$
\end_inset

 satisfies Walras' law, then for all 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \sum_{l=1}^{L}p_{l}\frac{\partial x_{l}(p,w)}{\partial p_{k}}+x_{k}(p,w)=0\ \mbox{for}\ k=1,\ldots,L}
\]

\end_inset

or, written in matrix notation,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p\cdot D_{p}x(p,w)+x(p,w)^{T}=0^{T}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Assume 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)$
\end_inset

 satisfies Walras' law.
 Then,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
p\cdot x(p,w) & = & w\nonumber \\
p_{1}x_{1}(p,w)+\ldots+p_{L}x_{L}(p,w) & = & w
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

Differentiating (2) with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{1}$
\end_inset

 yields:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\partial(p\cdot x(p,w))}{\partial p_{1}}=p_{1}\frac{\partial x_{1}(p,w)}{\partial p_{1}}+x_{1}(p,w)+p_{2}\frac{\partial x_{2}(p,w)}{\partial p_{1}}+\dots+p_{L}\frac{\partial x_{L}(p,w)}{\partial p_{1}}=0
\]

\end_inset

So, this implies that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \sum_{l=1}^{L}p_{l}\frac{\partial x_{l}(p,w)}{\partial p_{k}}+x_{k}(p,w)=0;\ \forall\ k=1,\ldots,L}
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We can also obtain the desired result using matrix notation.
 Define 
\begin_inset Formula $h(p):\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}\longrightarrow\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $h(p)=p$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $g(p):\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}\longrightarrow\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $g(p)=x(p,\bar{w})$
\end_inset

.
 Now, we can define 
\begin_inset Formula $f(p):\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}\longrightarrow\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $f(p)=h(p)\cdot g(p)$
\end_inset

.
 From (M.A.3) in the mathematical appendix of Mas-Colell,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
Df(p)=h(p)\cdot Dg(p)+g(p)\cdot Dh(p)
\]

\end_inset

From the product rule above, we obtain:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
D_{p}\left[p\cdot x(p,w)\right]=p\cdot D_{p}x(p,w)+x(p,w)^{T}
\]

\end_inset

Thus, the final result, in matrix notation, is expressed as:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p\cdot D_{p}x(p,w)+x(p,w)^{T}=0^{T}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*

\series bold
\shape up
2.E.3.

\series default
\emph on
 
\shape default
\emph default
If the Walrasian demand function 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)$
\end_inset

 satisfies Walras' law, then for all 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

:
