\begin_layout Proof
The preference 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is represented by the utility function 
\begin_inset Formula $U(x_{1},x_{2})=x_{1}^{\alpha}x_{2}^{1-\alpha}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof

\bar under
Part A.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We want to show that the preference is convex.
 Consider the increasing function 
\begin_inset Formula $V:\mathbb{R}^{2}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $V(x_{1},x_{2})=\alpha\ln x_{1}+(1-\alpha)\ln x_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 The Hessian matrix of this function is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
H=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
-\nicefrac{\alpha}{x_{1}^{2}} & 0\\
0 & -\nicefrac{(1-\alpha)}{x_{2}^{2}}
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset

which is negative definite.
 Since the Hessian matrix of 
\begin_inset Formula $V$
\end_inset

 is negative definite, then 
\begin_inset Formula $V$
\end_inset

 strictly concave.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $V$
\end_inset

 is stricly concave, then it is strictly quasi-concave.
 Now, consider the strictly increasing function 
\begin_inset Formula $f:\mathbb{R}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $f(x)=e^{x}$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is strictly increasing, then the composite function 
\begin_inset Formula $f\circ V$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $f\circ V=x_{1}^{\alpha}x_{2}^{1-\alpha}=U(x_{1},x_{2})$
\end_inset

, is strictly quasi-concave.
 By Proposition 8, 
\begin_inset Formula $U$
\end_inset

 is strictly quasi-concave if and only if 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is strictly convex.
 Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is not only convex, but it is actually strictly convex.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof

\bar under
Part B.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We want to show that for each 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{2}$
\end_inset

 and each 
\begin_inset Formula $w>0$
\end_inset

, there exists a unique maximizer for 
\begin_inset Formula $U$
\end_inset

 in the budget set 
\begin_inset Formula $B(p,w)$
\end_inset

 .
 Suppose for contradiction there exists 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{2}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $w>0$
\end_inset

 such that there is more than one maximizer for 
\begin_inset Formula $U$
\end_inset

 in 
\begin_inset Formula $B(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ x^{*},y^{*}\right\} \subseteq\arg\max_{B(p,w)}U(x_{1},x_{2})$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}\neq y^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y^{*}$
\end_inset

 are maximizers, then 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}\sim y^{*}$
\end_inset

, which is equivalent to 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}\succsim y^{*}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y^{*}\succsim x^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 Take 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha\in(0,1)$
\end_inset

.
 By construction, 
\begin_inset Formula $B(p,w)$
\end_inset

 is a convex set, so 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha x^{*}+(1-\alpha)y^{*}\in B(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
 Furthermore, by the strict convexity of 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

, since 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}\succsim y^{*}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}\neq y^{*}$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha x^{*}+(1-\alpha)y^{*}\succ y^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 However, this contradicts the fact that 
\begin_inset Formula $y^{*}$
\end_inset

 is a maximizer 
\begin_inset Formula $(\rightarrow\leftarrow)$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, for each 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{2}$
\end_inset

 and each 
\begin_inset Formula $w>0$
\end_inset

, there is a unique maximizer for 
\begin_inset Formula $U$
\end_inset

 in the budget set 
\begin_inset Formula $B(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof

\bar under
Part C.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

After maximizing 
\begin_inset Formula $U(x_{1},x_{2})$
\end_inset

 over 
\begin_inset Formula $B(p,w)$
\end_inset

, we obtain the unique maximizer, 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)$
\end_inset

, which is the demand function associated to 
\begin_inset Formula $U$
\end_inset

.
 An explicit expression for 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)$
\end_inset

 is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x(p,w)=\left[\begin{array}{c}
\frac{\alpha w}{p_{1}}\\
\frac{(1-\alpha)w}{p_{2}}
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset

Important to note, maximizing a utility function 
\begin_inset Formula $u:\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 of the form
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u(x)={\displaystyle \prod_{l=1}^{L}}x_{l}^{\alpha_{l}}
\]

\end_inset

 subject to 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{L}}p_{l}x_{l}\leq w
\]

\end_inset

leads to the following demand function
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x_{l}(p,w)=\frac{\alpha_{l}w}{\left(\sum_{l=1}^{L}\alpha_{l}\right)p_{l}}
\]

\end_inset

for all 
\begin_inset Formula $l=1,\ldots,L$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof

\bar under
Part D.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

For each 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,w)\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
C^{\succsim}(p,w)=\arg\max\{u(x):x\in B(p,w)\}
\]

\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is strictly convex, then its associated choice structure 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathcal{B}_{++}^{W},C^{\succsim})$
\end_inset

 is single-valued and 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)$
\end_inset

 is the unique element in 
\begin_inset Formula $C^{\succsim}(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
 By Proposition 5, 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathcal{B}_{++}^{W},C^{\succsim})$
\end_inset

 satisfies WARP, and by Proposition 11, its associated demand function 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)$
\end_inset

 satisfies the Compensated Law of Demand.
\end_layout

