\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(69).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\shape up

\begin_inset Formula $(\mathcal{B}^{W},C)$
\end_inset

 be a single-valued Walrasian choice structure and 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 its associated demand function.
 The 
\series bold
\shape default
Slutsky compensated demand of x at 
\begin_inset Formula $(\bar{p},\bar{w})\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

 
\series default
is the function
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\begin{array}{cccl}
x^{(\bar{p},\bar{w})}: & \mathbb{R}_{+}^{L} & \rightarrow & \mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}\\
 & p & \mapsto & x^{(\bar{p},\bar{w})}(p)\equiv x(p,p\cdot x(\bar{p},\bar{w}))\equiv\left[\begin{array}{c}
x_{1}(p,p\cdot x(\bar{p},\bar{w}))\\
\vdots\\
x_{L}(p,p\cdot x(\bar{p},\bar{w}))
\end{array}\right]
\end{array}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*

\series bold
\shape up
(12).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathcal{B}^{W},C)$
\end_inset

 be a single-valued Walrasian choice structure and 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 its associated demand function.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is differentiable, then for each 
\begin_inset Formula $(\bar{p},\bar{w})\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{(\bar{p},\bar{w})}(p)$
\end_inset

 is differentiable and for each 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

 and each 
\begin_inset Formula $\{l,k\}\subseteq\{1,\ldots,L\}$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\partial x_{l}^{(\bar{p},\bar{w})}(p,p\cdot x(\bar{p},\bar{w}))}{\partial p_{k}}=\frac{\partial x_{l}(p,p\cdot x(\bar{p},\bar{w}))}{\partial p_{k}}+\frac{\partial x_{l}(p,p\cdot x(\bar{p},\bar{w}))}{\partial w}x_{k}(p,p\cdot x(\bar{p},\bar{w}))
\]

\end_inset

or, in matrix notation,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
D_{p}x^{(\bar{p},\bar{w})}(p)=D_{p}x(p,p\cdot x(\bar{p},\bar{w}))+D_{w}(p,p\cdot x(\bar{p},\bar{w}))x(\bar{p},\bar{w})^{T}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Straightforward application of the chain rule given that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x_{l}^{(\bar{p},\bar{w})}(p)=x_{l}(p_{1},\ldots,p_{L},p_{1}x_{1}(\bar{p},\bar{w})+\cdots+p_{L}x_{L}(\bar{p},\bar{w}))
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Consider starting at a given price-wealth pair 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,w)$
\end_inset

, a differential change in prices 
\begin_inset Formula $dp$
\end_inset

.
 Imagine that we make this a compensated price change by giving the consumer
 compensation of 
\begin_inset Formula $dw=x(p,w)\cdot dp$
\end_inset

 (this is just the differential analog of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta w=x(p,w)\cdot\Delta p$
\end_inset

).
 Proposition 11 tells us that
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
dp\cdot dx\leq0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Now, using the chain rule, the differential change in demand induced by
 this compensated price change can be written as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
dx=D_{p}x(p,w)dp+D_{w}x(p,w)dw
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Hence
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
dx=D_{p}x(p,w)dp+D_{w}x(p,w)\left[x(p,w)\cdot dp\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset

or equivalently
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
dx=\left[D_{p}x(p,w)dp+D_{w}x(p,w)x(p,w)^{T}\right]dp
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Finally, substituting (6) into (3) we conclude that for any possible differentia
l price change 
\begin_inset Formula $dp$
\end_inset

, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
dp\cdot\left[D_{p}x(p,w)dp+D_{w}x(p,w)x(p,w)^{T}\right]dp\leq0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

The expression in square brackets in condition (7) is an 
\begin_inset Formula $L\times L$
\end_inset

 matrix, which we denote by 
\begin_inset Formula $S(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(70).

\series default
 
\shape up
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathcal{B}^{W},C)$
\end_inset

 be a single-valued Walrasian choice structure that satisfies Walras' law
 and such that its associated demand function 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is differentiable.
 The derivative of the Slutsky compensated demand of 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 at 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,w)\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

, evaluated at 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

, denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $S(p,w)$
\end_inset

, is called the 
\series bold
\shape default
substitution
\series default
, or 
\series bold
Slutsky
\series default
, matrix of 
\shape up

\begin_inset Formula $(\mathcal{B}^{W},C)$
\end_inset

 at 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
 That is, 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ (p,w)\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{+},\ \forall\ \{l,k\}\subseteq\{1,\ldots,L\}:$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
S_{lk}(p,w)=\frac{\partial x_{l}(p,w)}{\partial p_{k}}+\frac{\partial x_{l}(p,w)}{\partial w}x_{k}(p,w)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

or in matrix notation,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
S(p,w)=D_{p}x(p,w)+D_{w}x(p,w)x(p,w)^{T}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The elements of the 
\emph on
substitution
\emph default
, or 
\emph on
Slutsky
\emph default
, matrix are known as 
\emph on
substitution effects
\emph default
.
 The 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

substitution
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 terminology is apt because the term 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{lk}(p,w)$
\end_inset

 measures the differential change in the consumption of commodity 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 (i.e., the substitution to or from other commodities) due to a differential
 change in the price of commodity 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 when wealth is adjusted so that the consumer can still just afford his
 original bundle (i.e., due solely to a change in relative prices).
 To see this, note that the change in demand for good 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 if wealth is left unchanged is 
\begin_inset Formula $(\nicefrac{\partial x_{l}(p,w)}{\partial p_{k}})dp_{k}$
\end_inset

.
 For the consumer to be able to 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

just afford
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 his original consumption bundle, his wealth must vary by the amount 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{k}(p,w)dp_{k}$
\end_inset

.
 The effect of this wealth change on the demand for good 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 is then 
\begin_inset Formula $(\nicefrac{\partial x_{l}(p,w)}{\partial w})\left[x_{k}(p,w)dp_{k}\right]$
\end_inset

.
 The sum of these two effects is therefore exactly 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{lk}(p,w)dp_{k}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We summarize the derivation in equations (3) to (9) in Proposition 13.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*

\series bold
\shape up
(13).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathcal{B}^{W},C)$
\end_inset

 be a single-valued Walrasian choice structure that satisfies WARP, Walras'
 law, and its associated demand function 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is twice differentiable.
 Then, for each 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,w)\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

 and each 
\begin_inset Formula $v\in\mathbb{R}^{L}$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
v^{T}S(p,w)v\leq0
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon>0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\epsilon}\equiv p+\epsilon v$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $C^{1}$
\end_inset

, then by Taylor's formula,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x^{(p,w)}(p^{\epsilon})-x^{(p,w)}(p)=D_{p}x^{(p,w)}(p)(p^{\epsilon}-p)+R(p,p^{\epsilon})=S(p,w)(p^{\epsilon}-p)+R(p,p^{\epsilon})
\]

\end_inset

where
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \lim_{p^{\epsilon}\rightarrow p}}\frac{R(p,p^{\epsilon})}{\left\Vert p^{\epsilon}-p\right\Vert }=0
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathcal{B}^{W},C^{\succsim})$
\end_inset

 satisfies Walras' law, then 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{(p,w)}(p)=x(p,w)$
\end_inset

, so
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x^{(p,w)}(p^{\epsilon})-x^{(p,w)}(p)=x(p^{\epsilon},p^{\epsilon}\cdot x(p,w))-x(p,w)
\]

\end_inset

Thus,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left[p^{\epsilon}-p\right]^{T}\left[x(p^{\epsilon},p^{\epsilon}\cdot x(p,w))-x(p,w)\right]=\left[p^{\epsilon}-p\right]^{T}\left[S(p,w)\left[p^{\epsilon}-p\right]+R(p,p^{\epsilon})\right]
\]

\end_inset

By the Compensated Law of Demand,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left[p^{\epsilon}-p\right]^{T}\left[S(p,w)\left[p^{\epsilon}-p\right]+R(p,p^{\epsilon})\right]\leq0
\]

\end_inset

Recall that 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\epsilon}-p=\epsilon v.$
\end_inset

 Thus,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\epsilon v^{T}S(p,w)[\epsilon v]+\epsilon v^{T}R(p,p^{\epsilon})\leq0
\]

\end_inset

Dividing by 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon^{2}$
\end_inset

 and rearranging terms,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
v^{T}S(p,w)v\leq-\frac{v^{T}R(p,p^{\epsilon})}{\epsilon}
\]

\end_inset

Since the expression above holds for each 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon>0$
\end_inset

, then (whenever the limit of the expression the right exists)
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
v^{T}S(p,w)v\leq{\displaystyle \lim_{\epsilon\rightarrow0}}-\frac{v^{T}R(p,p^{\epsilon})}{\epsilon}
\]

\end_inset

Finally, since 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\Vert p^{\epsilon}-p\right\Vert =\epsilon\left\Vert v\right\Vert $
\end_inset

, then
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \lim_{\epsilon\rightarrow0}}-\frac{v^{T}R(p,p^{\epsilon})}{\epsilon}\frac{\left\Vert v\right\Vert }{\left\Vert v\right\Vert }=-\left\Vert v\right\Vert v^{T}{\displaystyle \lim_{\epsilon\rightarrow0}}\frac{R(p,p^{\epsilon})}{\left\Vert p^{\epsilon}-p\right\Vert }=0
\]

\end_inset

Thus,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
v^{T}S(p,w)v\leq0
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Corollary*

\series bold
\shape up
(2).

\series default
 
\emph on
If a single-valued choice structure satisfies Walras' law and WARP and its
 associated demand function is twice differentiable, then its Slutsky matrix
 is negative semi-definite.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Note*
Check Mas-Colell and Lemma 2.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

A matrix satisfying the property in Proposition 13 is called 
\emph on
negative semidefinite
\emph default
 (it is 
\emph on
negative
\emph default
 
\emph on
definite
\emph default
 if the inequality is strict for all 
\begin_inset Formula $v\neq0$
\end_inset

).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Note that 
\begin_inset Formula $S(p,w)$
\end_inset

 being negative semidefinite implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{ll}(p,w)\leq0$
\end_inset

: That is, 
\emph on
the substitution effect of good 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 with respect to its own price is always nonpositive
\emph default
.
 An interesting implication of 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{ll}(p,w)\leq0$
\end_inset

 is that a good can be a Giffen good at 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,w)$
\end_inset

 only if it is inferior.
 In particular, since
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
s_{ll}(p,w)=\frac{\partial x_{l}(p,w)}{\partial p_{l}}+\frac{\partial x_{l}(p,w)}{\partial w}x_{l}(p,w)\leq0
\]

\end_inset

if 
\begin_inset Formula $\nicefrac{\partial x_{l}(p,w)}{\partial p_{l}}>0$
\end_inset

, we must have 
\begin_inset Formula $\nicefrac{\partial x_{l}(p,w)}{\partial w}<0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

For later reference, we note that Proposition 13 does 
\emph on
not
\emph default
 imply, in general, that the matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $S(p,w)$
\end_inset

 is symmetric.
 For 
\begin_inset Formula $L=2$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $S(p,w)$
\end_inset

 is necessarily symmetric.
 When 
\begin_inset Formula $L>2$
\end_inset

, however, 
\begin_inset Formula $S(p,w)$
\end_inset

 need not be symmetric under the assumptions made so far (homogeneity of
 degree zero, Walras' law, and the weak axiom).
 In the next two sections, we shall see that the symmetry of 
\begin_inset Formula $S(p,w)$
\end_inset

 is intimately connected with the possibility of generating demand from
 the maximization of rational preferences.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Exploiting further the properties of homogeneity of degree zero and Walras'
 law, we can say a bit more about the substitution matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $S(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

