\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Propostion 13 establishes negative semidefiniteness of 
\begin_inset Formula $S(p,w)$
\end_inset

 as a necessary implication of the weak axiom.
 One might wonder: Is this property sufficient to imply the weak axiom (so
 that the negative semidefiniteness of 
\begin_inset Formula $S(p,w)$
\end_inset

 is actually equivalent to the WA)? That is, if we have a demand function
 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)$
\end_inset

 that satisfies Walras' law, homogeneity of degree zero and has a negative
 semidefinite substitution matrix, must it satisfy the weak axiom? The answer
 is 
\emph on
almost
\emph default
, 
\emph on
but not quite
\emph default
.
 The sufficient condition is that 
\begin_inset Formula $v\cdot S(p,w)v<0$
\end_inset

 whenever 
\begin_inset Formula $v\neq\alpha p$
\end_inset

 for any scalar 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

; that is, 
\begin_inset Formula $S(p,w)$
\end_inset

 must be negative definite for all vectors other than those that are proportiona
l to 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

.
 The gap between the necessary and sufficient conditions is of the same
 nature as the gap between the necessary and the sufficient second-order
 conditions for the minimization of a funciton.
\end_layout

