\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Finally, how would a theory of consumer demand that is based solely on the
 assumptions of homogeneity of degree zero, Walras' law, and the consistency
 requirement embodied in the weak axiom compare with one based on rational
 preference maximization? The two theories are, in fact, not equivalent.
 For Walrasian demand functions, the theory derived from the weak axiom
 is weaker than the theory derived from rational preferences, in the sense
 of implying fewer restrictions.
 This is shown formally in the next two sections, where we demonstrate that
 if demand is generated from perferences, or is capable of being so generated,
 then it must have a symmetric Slutsky matrix at all 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Note*
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{B}^{W}$
\end_inset

 does not satisfy the pair-and-triple condition, so a demand function induced
 by choice, 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)$
\end_inset

, that satisfies WARP may not have been induced by the maximization of preferenc
es.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
3.4.6
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Strong Axiom of Revealed Preferences: SARP
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathcal{B}^{W},C)$
\end_inset

 be a single-valued choice structure that satisfies Walras' law and WARP.
 Are there preferences on 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 that rationalize such a choice structure? We have seen that that in the
 context of consumer demand theory, consumer choice may satisfy the weak
 axiom but not be capable of being generated by a rational preference relation
 (this is possible because 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{B}^{W}$
\end_inset

 does not satisfy the pair-and-triple condition).
 This will be clear after characterizing the choice structures induced by
 maximization of preferences.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We could therefore ask: Can we find a necessary and sufficient consistency
 condition on consumer demand behavior that is in the same style as the
 weak axiom, but that does imply that demand behavior can be rationalized
 by preferences? The answer is 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

yes,
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 and the following stronger consistency condition guarantees that a single-value
d choice structure that satisfies Walras' law can be rationalized by a preferenc
e relation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(71).

\series default
 
\emph on
The demand function 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 satisfies the 
\series bold
Strong Axiom of Revealed Preference (SARP)
\series default
 iff for each list 
\begin_inset Formula $(p^{1},w^{1}),\ldots,(p^{M},w^{M})$
\end_inset

, such that for each 
\begin_inset Formula $m\in\{1,\ldots,M-1\}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{m}\cdot x(p^{m+1},w^{m+1})\leq w^{m}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p^{m+1},w^{m+1})\neq x(p^{m},w^{m})$
\end_inset

, we have that 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{M}\cdot x(p^{1},w^{1})>w^{M}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*

\series bold
\shape up
(16).

\series default
 WARP implies SARP when there are exactly two commodities (i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $L=2$
\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathcal{B}^{W},C)$
\end_inset

 be a single-valued choice structure that satisfies Walras' law.
 Assume 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathcal{B}^{W},C)$
\end_inset

 satisfies WARP.
 We want to show that its associated demand function 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 satisfies SARP.
 When 
\begin_inset Formula $L=2$
\end_inset

, the definition of SARP is as follows: 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ (p^{1},w^{1}),\ \forall\ (p^{2},w^{2}):\left[p^{1}\cdot x(p^{2},w^{2})\leq w^{1}\ \wedge\ x(p^{1},w^{1})\neq x(p^{2},w^{2})\right]\implies p^{2}\cdot x(p^{1},w^{1})>w^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 By Proposition 10, WARP is equivalent to the previous statement.
 Therefore, when 
\begin_inset Formula $L=2$
\end_inset

, WARP is equivalent to SARP.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Hence, by Proposition 16, when 
\begin_inset Formula $L=2$
\end_inset

 and demand satisfies the weak axiom, we can always find a rationalizing
 preference relation.
 When 
\begin_inset Formula $L>2$
\end_inset

, however, the strong axiom is stronger than the weak axiom.
 In fact, the choice-based theory of demand founded on the strong axiom
 is essentially equivalent to the preference-based theory of demand presented
 in the subsequent sections.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The strong axiom is therefore essentially equivalent both to the rational
 preference hypothesis and to the symmetry and negative semidefiniteness
 of the Slutsky matrix (which will be proven later).
 We have seen that the weak axiom is essentially equivalent to the negative
 semidefiniteness of the Slutsky matrix.
 It is therefore natural to ask whether there is an assumption on preferences
 that is weaker than rationality and that leads to a theory of consumer
 demand equivalent to that based on the weak axiom.
 Violations of the strong axiom mean cycling choice, and violations of the
 symmetry of the Slutsky matrix generate path dependence in attempts to
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

integrate back
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 to preferences.
 This suggests preferences that may violate the transitivity axiom.
\end_layout

