\begin_layout Problem
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $X\equiv\mathbb{R}_{+}^{3}$
\end_inset

.
 Suppose that a Walrasian demand function 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 satisfies Walras' law and is such that for each pair 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,w)\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{3}\times\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
x_{1}(p,w) & \equiv & a+b\frac{p_{1}}{p_{3}}+c\frac{p_{2}}{p_{3}}\\
x_{2}(p,w) & \equiv & d+e\frac{p_{1}}{p_{3}}+f\frac{p_{2}}{p_{3}}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Calculate 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{3}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Is 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 homogenous of degree zero?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Provide restrictions on the demand parameters that guarantee the associated
 choice structrue satisfies WARP.
 (Disregard the fact that our results were developed under the assumpion
 
\begin_inset Formula $X\equiv\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Proof
First, we calculate 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{3}$
\end_inset

.
 Since the demand function 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 satisfies Walras' law, then
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p_{1}\cdot x_{1}(p,w)+p_{2}\cdot x_{2}(p,w)+p_{3}\cdot x_{3}(p,w)=w
\]

\end_inset

Solving for 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{3}$
\end_inset

 yields
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x_{3}(p,w)=\frac{w}{p_{3}}-\frac{p_{1}}{p_{3}}\cdot x_{1}(p,w)-\frac{p_{2}}{p_{3}}\cdot x_{2}(p,w)
\]

\end_inset

Plugging in the expressions for 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}(p,w)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}(p,w)$
\end_inset

, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x_{3}(p,w)=\frac{w}{p_{3}}-\frac{p_{1}}{p_{3}}\left(a+b\frac{p_{1}}{p_{3}}+c\frac{p_{2}}{p_{3}}\right)-\frac{p_{2}}{p_{3}}\left(d+e\frac{p_{1}}{p_{3}}+f\frac{p_{2}}{p_{3}}\right)
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Next, we prove that 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is homogenous of degree zero.
 Take 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha\in\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
x_{1}(\alpha p,\alpha w)=a+b\frac{\alpha p_{1}}{\alpha p_{3}}+c\frac{\alpha p_{2}}{\alpha p_{3}}=a+b\frac{p_{1}}{p_{3}}+c\frac{p_{2}}{p_{3}}=x_{1}(p,w)\\
x_{2}(\alpha p,\alpha w)=d+e\frac{\alpha p_{1}}{\alpha p_{3}}+f\frac{\alpha p_{2}}{\alpha p_{3}}=d+e\frac{p_{1}}{p_{3}}+f\frac{p_{2}}{p_{3}}=x_{2}(p,w)\\
x_{3}(\alpha p,\alpha w)=\frac{\alpha w}{\alpha p_{3}}-\frac{\alpha p_{1}}{\alpha p_{3}}\left(a+b\frac{\alpha p_{1}}{\alpha p_{3}}+c\frac{\alpha p_{2}}{\alpha p_{3}}\right)-\frac{\alpha p_{2}}{\alpha p_{3}}\left(d+e\frac{\alpha p_{1}}{\alpha p_{3}}+f\frac{\alpha p_{2}}{\alpha p_{3}}\right)\\
=\frac{w}{p_{3}}-\frac{p_{1}}{p_{3}}\left(a+b\frac{p_{1}}{p_{3}}+c\frac{p_{2}}{p_{3}}\right)-\frac{p_{2}}{p_{3}}\left(d+e\frac{p_{1}}{p_{3}}+f\frac{p_{2}}{p_{3}}\right)=x_{3}(p,w)
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \alpha\in\mathbb{R}_{+},\ \forall\ (p,w)\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{3}\times\mathbb{R}_{+}:x(\alpha p,\alpha w)=x(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is homogeneous of degree zero.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Finally, we provide restrictions on the demand parameters that guarantee
 the associated choice structure satisfies WARP.
 By assumption, the choice structure is single-valued and satisfies Walras'
 law.
 In addition, it can be shown that its associated demand function 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is twice differentiable.
 So, by Proposition 15, if the symmetric matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{l}+S_{l}^{T}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{l}$
\end_inset

 is the matrix obtained from 
\begin_inset Formula $S(p,w)$
\end_inset

 by deleting the 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

-th row and 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

-th column for some 
\begin_inset Formula $l\in\{1,\ldots,L\}$
\end_inset

, is negative definite, then 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathcal{B}^{W},C)$
\end_inset

 satisfies WARP.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{3}$
\end_inset

 be the matrix obtained from 
\begin_inset Formula $S(p,w)$
\end_inset

 be deleting the 3rd row and 3rd column.
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
S_{3}=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
\frac{b}{p_{3}} & \frac{c}{p_{3}}\\
\frac{e}{p_{3}} & \frac{f}{p_{3}}
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset

This yields
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
S_{3}+S_{3}^{T}=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
2\frac{b}{p_{3}} & \frac{c+e}{p_{3}}\\
\frac{c+e}{p_{3}} & 2\frac{f}{p_{3}}
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset

According to Theorem 11 in Section 3.4.7, 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{3}+S_{3}^{T}$
\end_inset

 is negative definite if and only if 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{11}<0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{det}(S_{3}+S_{3}^{T})>0$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $b<0$
\end_inset

 and
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
4\frac{bf}{p_{3}^{2}}-\frac{(c+e)^{2}}{p_{3}^{2}}>0\\
2\sqrt{bf}>(c+e)
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

Moreover, for 
\begin_inset Formula $\sqrt{bf}$
\end_inset

 to be well-defined, 
\begin_inset Formula $f<0$
\end_inset

 since 
\begin_inset Formula $b<0$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, putting these three restrictions on the demand parameters guarantees
 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathcal{B}^{W},C)$
\end_inset

 satisfies WARP.
\end_layout

