\begin_layout Problem
Consider a two-commodity consumption space 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}_{+}^{2}$
\end_inset

 in which the deneric demand function is denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
 We say that demand function 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 satisfies the 
\series bold
weak gross substitutes property (WGSP)
\series default
 if the following two conditions are satisfied:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
For each 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{1}\in\mathbb{R}_{++}$
\end_inset

 and each 
\begin_inset Formula $w\in\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

, the function 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{2}\mapsto x_{1}(p_{1},p_{2},w)$
\end_inset

 is non-decreasing.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
For each 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{2}\in\mathbb{R}_{++}$
\end_inset

 and each 
\begin_inset Formula $w\in\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

, the function 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{1}\mapsto x_{2}(p_{1},p_{2},w)$
\end_inset

 is non-decreasing.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Problem
Assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is homogenous of degree zero, satisfies Walras' law and WGSP.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\{p,p^{\prime}\}\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{++}^{2}$
\end_inset

 be such that 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{1}^{\prime}\geq p_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{2}^{\prime}\leq p_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Prove that 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}(p^{\prime},1)\leq x_{1}(p,1)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}(p^{\prime},1)\geq x_{2}(p,1)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Prove that if 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 satisfies WGSP then it satisfies WARP.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Does WARP imply WGSP?
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Proof
Assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is homogenous of degree zero, satisfies Walras' law and WGSP.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof

\bar under
Part A.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\{p,p^{\prime}\}\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{++}^{2}$
\end_inset

 be such that 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{1}^{\prime}\geq p_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{2}^{\prime}\leq p_{2}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

First, we prove that 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}(p^{\prime},1)\leq x_{1}(p,1)$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{2}^{\prime}\leq p_{2}$
\end_inset

, then by WGSP, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}(p_{1}^{\prime},p_{2}^{\prime},1)\leq x_{1}(p_{1}^{\prime},p_{2},1)$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 satisfies Walras' Law, then
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p_{1}^{\prime}x_{1}(p_{1}^{\prime},p_{2},1)+p_{2}x_{2}(p_{1}^{\prime},p_{2},1)=1=p_{1}x_{1}(p_{1},p_{2},1)+p_{2}x_{2}(p_{1},p_{2},1)
\]

\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{1}^{\prime}\geq p_{1}$
\end_inset

, then by WGSP, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}(p_{1}^{\prime},p_{2},1)\geq x_{2}(p_{1},p_{2},1)$
\end_inset

, so 
\begin_inset Formula $ $
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $p_{2}x_{2}(p_{1}^{\prime},p_{2},1)\geq p_{2}x_{2}(p_{1},p_{2},1)$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{1}^{\prime}\geq p_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{2}x_{2}(p_{1}^{\prime},p_{2},1)\geq p_{2}x_{2}(p_{1},p_{2},1)$
\end_inset

, then for the equation above to be satisfied, we must have 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}(p_{1}^{\prime},p_{2},1)\leq x_{1}(p_{1},p_{2},1)$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}(p^{\prime},1)\leq x_{1}(p_{1}^{\prime},p_{2},1)\leq x_{1}(p,1)$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}(p^{\prime},1)\leq x_{1}(p,1)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Next, we prove that 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}(p^{\prime},1)\geq x_{2}(p,1)$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{1}^{\prime}\geq p_{1}$
\end_inset

, then by WGSP, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}(p_{1}^{\prime},p_{2},1)\geq x_{2}(p_{1},p_{2},1)$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 satisfies Walras' Law, then
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p_{1}^{\prime}x_{1}(p_{1}^{\prime},p_{2}^{\prime},1)+p_{2}^{\prime}x_{2}(p_{1}^{\prime},p_{2}^{\prime},1)=1=p_{1}^{\prime}x_{1}(p_{1}^{\prime},p_{2},1)+p_{2}x(p_{1}^{\prime},p_{2},1)
\]

\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{2}^{\prime}\leq p_{2}$
\end_inset

, then by WGSP, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}(p_{1}^{\prime},p_{2}^{\prime},1)\leq x_{1}(p_{1}^{\prime},p_{2},1)$
\end_inset

, so 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{1}^{\prime}x_{1}(p_{1}^{\prime},p_{2}^{\prime},1)\leq p_{1}^{\prime}x_{1}(p_{1}^{\prime},p_{2},1)$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{2}^{\prime}\leq p_{2}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $ $
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $p_{1}^{\prime}x_{1}(p_{1}^{\prime},p_{2}^{\prime},1)\leq p_{1}^{\prime}x_{1}(p_{1}^{\prime},p_{2},1)$
\end_inset

, then for the equation above to be satisfied, we must have 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}(p_{1}^{\prime},p_{2}^{\prime},1)\geq x_{2}(p_{1}^{\prime},p_{2},1)$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}(p^{\prime},1)\geq x_{2}(p_{1}^{\prime},p_{2},1)\geq x_{2}(p,1)$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}(p^{\prime},1)\geq x_{2}(p,1)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof

\bar under
Part B.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Assume 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 satisfies WGSP.
 We want to show that its associated choice structure satisfies WARP.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is homogenous of degree zero and satisfies Walras' Law, then WARP is equivalent
 to 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 satisfying the following property by the result of Problem 9(B), Section
 3.4.8: 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \{p,p^{\prime}\}\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{++}^{2}:p^{\prime}\cdot x(p,1)<1\implies p\cdot x(p^{\prime},1)>1$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, to prove WARP, we will show that 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 satisfies this property.
 Take 
\family roman
\series medium
\shape up
\size normal
\emph off
\bar no
\strikeout off
\uuline off
\uwave off
\noun off
\color none

\begin_inset Formula $\{p,p^{\prime}\}\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{++}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Suppose that 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\prime}\cdot x(p,1)<1$
\end_inset

.
 We want to show that 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x(p^{\prime},1)>1$
\end_inset

.
 To do this, we analyze three cases.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Case
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\prime}\geq p$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 satisfies Walras' Law, then 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x(p,1)=1$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\prime}\geq p$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\prime}\cdot x(p,1)\geq1$
\end_inset

.
 However, this contradicts our supposition that 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\prime}\cdot x(p,1)<1$
\end_inset

.
 Hence, in this case, WARP is trivially satisfied.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Case
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\prime}\ll p$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 satisfies Walras' Law, then 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x(p,1)=1$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\prime}\ll p$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\prime}\cdot x(p,1)<1$
\end_inset

, so our supposition still holds.
 In addition, by Walras' Law, we have 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\prime}\cdot x(p^{\prime},1)=1$
\end_inset

.
 So, under 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\prime}\ll p$
\end_inset

, we have 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x(p^{\prime},1)>1$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, WARP is satisfied in this case.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Case
\begin_inset Formula $p_{2}^{\prime}<p_{2}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{1}^{\prime}\geq p_{1}$
\end_inset

.
 (Important to note, the case where 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{2}^{\prime}\geq p_{2}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{1}^{\prime}<p_{1}$
\end_inset

 is symmetric to this one).
 Then, by the results of Problem 10(A), Section 3.4.8, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}(p^{\prime},1)\leq x_{1}(p,1)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}(p^{\prime},1)\geq x_{2}(p,1)$
\end_inset

.
 Now, to summarize our results thus far, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
\Delta p_{1}\equiv p_{1}^{\prime}-p_{1}\geq0\\
\Delta x_{1}\equiv(x_{1}(p^{\prime},1)-x_{1}(p,1))\leq0\\
\Delta p_{2}\equiv p_{2}^{\prime}-p_{2}<0\\
\Delta x_{2}\equiv(x_{2}(p^{\prime},1)-x_{2}(p,1))\geq0
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

Under these four inequalities, we observe that 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\Delta p\cdot\Delta x=\Delta p_{1}\Delta x_{1}+\Delta p_{2}\Delta x_{2}\leq0
\]

\end_inset

Rewriting 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta p\cdot\Delta x$
\end_inset

 yields
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
(p^{\prime}-p)\cdot(x^{\prime}-x)=p^{\prime}\cdot x^{\prime}-p^{\prime}\cdot x-p\cdot x^{\prime}+p\cdot x\leq0
\]

\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 satisfies Walras' Law, then 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\prime}\cdot x^{\prime}=1$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x=1$
\end_inset

.
 So, the previous inequality becomes
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
2\leq p\cdot x^{\prime}+p^{\prime}\cdot x
\]

\end_inset

Finally, since 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\prime}\cdot x<1$
\end_inset

 by our supposition, then this yields
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
2\leq p\cdot x^{\prime}+p^{\prime}\cdot x<p\cdot x^{\prime}+1
\]

\end_inset

which implies that
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
2 & < & p\cdot x^{\prime}+1\\
1 & < & p\cdot x^{\prime}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Thus, WARP is satisfied in this case.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Proof
Since both 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 were arbitrary prices, then we have proved the desired property.
 Therefore, by the result of Problem 9(B), Problem 3.4.8, WARP is satisfied.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof

\bar under
Part C.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Does WARP imply WGSP? No, it does not.
 Consider the case of perfect complements.
 Suppose that the consumer's preferences are represented by the utility
 function 
\begin_inset Formula $u(x_{1},x_{2})=\min\{x_{1},x_{2}\}$
\end_inset

.
 The demand correspondence associated with the maximization of preferences
 will be single-valued, and it will satisfy WARP by Proposition 5.
 However, the consumer will purchases equal amounts of both goods.
 Consequently, and increase in 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{1}$
\end_inset

 will cause the consumer to purcahse less of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 This means that the demand function fails to satisfy WGSP.
\end_layout

